  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Eastern Air Express has $224M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 61 awards with an average award size of $3.7M. Obligations are up 3870.1% year over year. Contract activity increased sharply from prior fiscal years: FY25 obligations were $5.6M, FY24 obligations were $2.2M, FY23 obligations were $2.9M, FY22 obligations were $3.3K, and FY21 obligations were $0. The Department of Homeland Security is the dominant buyer, with $216M in obligations to Eastern Air Express in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense and the Department of the Interior follow, with $5.6M and $3M respectively. The current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date obligations. Large year-over-year growth and concentration in one buying agency are the clearest features of the FY2026 picture for Eastern Air Express.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main federal buyers for Eastern Air Express?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security obligated $216M to Eastern Air Express in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $5.6M and the Department of the Interior obligated $3M. The three agencies account for the bulk of FY2026 obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has Eastern Air Express's federal business changed over time?",
        "body": "Eastern Air Express had $0 in FY21 and grew to $3.3K in FY22, $2.9M in FY23, $2.2M in FY24, and $5.6M in FY25. FY2026 to date obligations jumped to $224M. Obligations are up 3870.1% year over year."
      }
    ]
  },
